{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-47650" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981111/assets/8e7daf95fadbe2262a09e8183f00d63075655f3b97dd363984c192ac3414ad17.png)\n\nConsider the function $y=f(x)$ whose graph is plotted above. At which points is the function $f(x)$ **not** differentiable?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981111/state.json" "file_sha256" "026cde3f17d40231e3117e7ee3da108aaaa65a5793ef3952d4134e9700353508"} {"question" "q-47650" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The function is not differentiable wherever it has a discontinuity, corner, cusp, or vertical tangent.\n\n- At $x=-2$ the function has a cusp, so it's not differentiable there.\n\n- At $x=0$ the function has a jump discontinuity, so it's not differentiable there.\n\n- At $x=2$ the function has an infinite discontinuity, so it's not differentiable there.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $x=-2$, $x=0$, and $x=2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981111/state.json" "file_sha256" "026cde3f17d40231e3117e7ee3da108aaaa65a5793ef3952d4134e9700353508"} {"question" "q-47650" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981111/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "451b510a06bca60a0c28313f73aa2bc718e238c19e6af8e690945b52308e618a"} {"question" "q-47650" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981111/state.json" "file_sha256" "026cde3f17d40231e3117e7ee3da108aaaa65a5793ef3952d4134e9700353508"} {"question" "q-47650" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$x=-2$ and $x=2$ only"] ["text" "$x=-2$ only"] ["text" "$x=-2$, $x=0$, and $x=2$"] ["text" "$x=0$ only"] ["text" "$x=2$ only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981111/state.json" "file_sha256" "026cde3f17d40231e3117e7ee3da108aaaa65a5793ef3952d4134e9700353508"} {"question" "q-47650" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$x=-2$, $x=0$, and $x=2$"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981111/state.json" "file_sha256" "026cde3f17d40231e3117e7ee3da108aaaa65a5793ef3952d4134e9700353508"} {"question" "q-47678" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following functions is differentiable at the point $x=3?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981111/state.json" "file_sha256" "026cde3f17d40231e3117e7ee3da108aaaa65a5793ef3952d4134e9700353508"} {"question" "q-47678" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Notice that the function $p(x)$ is differentiable for all $x>0.$ Therefore, the correct answer is $p(x)=\\sqrt{x}.$\n\nOther functions are not differentiable at $x=3.$\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not defined at $x=3$, so it is not differentiable there.\n\n- The function $g(x)$ has a corner point at $x=3$, so it is not differentiable there.\n\n- The function $h(x)$ has a vertical tangent at  $x=3$, so it is not differentiable there.\n\n- The function $q(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x=3$, so it is not differentiable there." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981111/state.json" "file_sha256" "026cde3f17d40231e3117e7ee3da108aaaa65a5793ef3952d4134e9700353508"} {"question" "q-47678" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981111/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "451b510a06bca60a0c28313f73aa2bc718e238c19e6af8e690945b52308e618a"} {"question" "q-47678" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981111/state.json" "file_sha256" "026cde3f17d40231e3117e7ee3da108aaaa65a5793ef3952d4134e9700353508"} {"question" "q-47678" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "f(x)=\\frac{3}{\\sqrt[3]{x-3}}"] ["math" "g(x)=3|x-3|"] ["math" "h(x)=\\sqrt[3]{x-3}"] ["math" "p(x)=\\sqrt{x}"] ["math" "q(x)={\\begin{aligned}x-3, & x<3 \\\\ x+3, & x\\ge 3\\end{aligned}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981111/state.json" "file_sha256" "026cde3f17d40231e3117e7ee3da108aaaa65a5793ef3952d4134e9700353508"} {"question" "q-47678" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "p(x)=\\sqrt{x}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981111/state.json" "file_sha256" "026cde3f17d40231e3117e7ee3da108aaaa65a5793ef3952d4134e9700353508"} {"question" "q-51067" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that a function $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=-2$, has a cusp at $x=1,$ and is differentiable at $x=3$, which of the following statements must be true for **any** $f(x)$ that satisfy these criteria?\n\n- It is differentiable at $x=-2$\n- It's **not** differentiable at $x=1$\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 3}f(x)=f(3).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981111/state.json" "file_sha256" "026cde3f17d40231e3117e7ee3da108aaaa65a5793ef3952d4134e9700353508"} {"question" "q-51067" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "- Statement I is false. Continuity does not imply differentiability. For example, the function $y=|x+2|$ is continuous but it's not differentiable at $x=-2.$\n\n- Statement II is true. The function has a cusp at $x=1$, so it's not differentiable there.\n\n- Statement III is true. Differentiability implies continuity. Hence, ${lim}_{x\\to 3}f(x)=f(3).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981111/state.json" "file_sha256" "026cde3f17d40231e3117e7ee3da108aaaa65a5793ef3952d4134e9700353508"} {"question" "q-51067" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981111/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "451b510a06bca60a0c28313f73aa2bc718e238c19e6af8e690945b52308e618a"} {"question" "q-51067" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981111/state.json" "file_sha256" "026cde3f17d40231e3117e7ee3da108aaaa65a5793ef3952d4134e9700353508"} {"question" "q-51067" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981111/state.json" "file_sha256" "026cde3f17d40231e3117e7ee3da108aaaa65a5793ef3952d4134e9700353508"} {"question" "q-51067" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II and III only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981111/state.json" "file_sha256" "026cde3f17d40231e3117e7ee3da108aaaa65a5793ef3952d4134e9700353508"} {"question" "q-101991" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981111/assets/6ec1bb6f164aa4878480f06707722769d1e17ff735a04c29a3fc2b9bd09ca467.png)\n\nConsider the function $y=f(x)$ whose graph is plotted above. At which points is the function $f(x)$ **not** differentiable?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981111/state.json" "file_sha256" "026cde3f17d40231e3117e7ee3da108aaaa65a5793ef3952d4134e9700353508"} {"question" "q-101991" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The function is not differentiable wherever it has a discontinuity, corner, cusp, or vertical tangent.\n\n- At $x=-10$ the function has a corner, so it's not differentiable there.\n\n- At $x=-5$ the function has an infinite discontinuity, so it's not differentiable there.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $x=-10$ and $x=-5.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981111/state.json" "file_sha256" "026cde3f17d40231e3117e7ee3da108aaaa65a5793ef3952d4134e9700353508"} {"question" "q-101991" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981111/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "451b510a06bca60a0c28313f73aa2bc718e238c19e6af8e690945b52308e618a"} {"question" "q-101991" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981111/state.json" "file_sha256" "026cde3f17d40231e3117e7ee3da108aaaa65a5793ef3952d4134e9700353508"} {"question" "q-101991" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$x=-10$ and $x=-5$ only"] ["text" "$x=-10$ only"] ["text" "$x=-10,$ $x=-5,$  and $x=0$"] ["text" "$x=-5$ only"] ["text" "$x=0$ only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981111/state.json" "file_sha256" "026cde3f17d40231e3117e7ee3da108aaaa65a5793ef3952d4134e9700353508"} {"question" "q-101991" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$x=-10$ and $x=-5$ only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981111/state.json" "file_sha256" "026cde3f17d40231e3117e7ee3da108aaaa65a5793ef3952d4134e9700353508"} {"question" "q-102163" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function given below?\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}2x-2, & x<0 \\\\ -2, & x\\ge 0\\end{aligned}\\,\n$$\n\n- It is continuous at $x=0$\n- It is differentiable at $x=0$\n- It is differentiable on the interval $(0,2)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981111/state.json" "file_sha256" "026cde3f17d40231e3117e7ee3da108aaaa65a5793ef3952d4134e9700353508"} {"question" "q-102163" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "- Statement I is true  Indeed, let's investigate the continuity of $f(x).$ It is continuous everywhere except, possibly, at  $x=0.$ At this point, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}(2x-2)=2(0)-2=-2 \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}-2=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x)=f(0)=-2,\n$$\n\nand so $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=0$ .\n\n- Statement II is false, $f(x)$ is not differentiable at $x=0$ because it has a corner point there: \n\n$\\,$ ${f}^{′}(x)=2\\,$  if  $\\,x<0$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0\\,$ if $\\,x>0$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, $f(x)$ is differentiable everywhere except at $x=0.$   Since $x=0$  doesn't belong to $(0,2)$, the given function is differentiable on this interval.\n\nTherefore, only statements I and III are true." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981111/state.json" "file_sha256" "026cde3f17d40231e3117e7ee3da108aaaa65a5793ef3952d4134e9700353508"} {"question" "q-102163" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981111/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "451b510a06bca60a0c28313f73aa2bc718e238c19e6af8e690945b52308e618a"} {"question" "q-102163" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981111/state.json" "file_sha256" "026cde3f17d40231e3117e7ee3da108aaaa65a5793ef3952d4134e9700353508"} {"question" "q-102163" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981111/state.json" "file_sha256" "026cde3f17d40231e3117e7ee3da108aaaa65a5793ef3952d4134e9700353508"} {"question" "q-102163" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981111/state.json" "file_sha256" "026cde3f17d40231e3117e7ee3da108aaaa65a5793ef3952d4134e9700353508"}] "usage" {"q-47650" [] "q-47678" []} "basis" 880 "retractions" [[17592186115794 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1691/Source/Images/